       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Role in hypertensive emergency:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " short-acting IV drugs (eg, labetalol, clevidipine, esmolol); labetalol vasodilates without reflex tachycardia and is used in pregnancy and for intracranial disorders."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Labetalol IV for severe HTN:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " 20 mg slow IV over 2 minutes, then 40 or 80 mg every 10 minutes up to 300 mg total, or an infusion of 2 mg/min; check supine BP at 5 and 10 minutes after each injection."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Interactions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " digoxin, clonidine, diltiazem and verapamil add bradycardia (slowed AV conduction); stopping clonidine: withdraw the beta-blocker several days first (rebound HTN). Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine, quinidine) can double metoprolol levels. Higher epinephrine doses may be needed."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overdose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " severe bradycardia, hypotension, AV block, bronchospasm and cardiogenic shock. Treat bradycardia with atropine, adrenergic drugs or pacing; hypotension with IV vasopressors; shock with glucagon (bolus, then infusion if needed). Hemodialysis is unlikely to help with metoprolol."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
